> I have started a plan to build an abstraction around model and tool selection in my AI system. Here is the contract for tools:
> """
> Tool protocol and normalized typed tool-result contract.
> 
> Tools are executable capabilities. In the initial synchronous system they may
> be invoked directly by a responder. In the workflow system they are executed
> as Operations and yield Artifacts. Tool interfaces should therefore avoid
> assuming that their primary result is user-visible text.
> 
> Expected tool callable shape:
> 
> ToolCallable = Callable[
>     [ToolInvocation],
>     ToolResult,
> ]
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class ToolInvocation:
>     operation_id: str | None
>     conversation_id: str
>     arguments: Mapping[str, Any]
>     config: Mapping[str, Any]
>     context: PlannerContext | None
>     provenance_requirements: Mapping[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Tool:
>     name: str
>     description: str
>     fn: ToolCallable | Callable[..., Any]
>     config: ToolConfig
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
>     version: str = "1"
> 
> Compatibility:
> - registry.py may accept legacy functions such as
>   `fn(prompt, config) -> str`.
> - tools/base.py normalizes such outputs to ToolResult/Artifact.
> - Tool configuration defaults and allowed discrete options are validated here.
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py or workflow/workers.py invokes:
>       result = tool.fn(invocation)
> - the caller persists raw temporary output as appropriate, converts relevant
>   materials to artifacts/source excerpts, and never blindly injects untrusted
>   tool text into system instructions.
> """
> 
> Now, here is the documentation for a tool available through OpenAI:
> ## Web search
> 
> `web_search` lets the model search and open pages, returning a `web_search_call` item plus a message with `url_citation` annotations. The older `web_search_preview` still works but ignores the newer options.
> 
> ```python
> resp = client.responses.create(
>     model="gpt-6-astra",
>     tools=[{
>         "type": "web_search",
>         "search_context_size": "high",
>         "filters": {"allowed_domains": ["www.who.int", "www.cdc.gov"]},
>         "user_location": {"type": "approximate", "country": "CA",
>                           "city": "Toronto", "region": "Ontario",
>                           "timezone": "America/Toronto"},
>         "external_web_access": True,
>     }],
>     include=["web_search_call.action.sources"],
>     input="Latest WHO guidance on heat-health action plans?",
> )
> ```
> 
> | Option | Values / range | Default |
> | --- | --- | --- |
> | `search_context_size` | `low`, `medium`, `high` | `medium` |
> | `filters.allowed_domains` | up to 100 domains, no `https://` prefix; subdomains included | none |
> | `filters.blocked_domains` | up to 100 domains | none |
> | `user_location` | `type: "approximate"` + `country` (ISO 2-letter), `city`, `region` (free text), `timezone` (IANA) | none |
> | `external_web_access` | `true` live fetch, `false` cache/index only | `true` |
> | `return_token_budget` | `default` or `unlimited` (GPT-5+ reasoning models only) | `default` |
> | `search_content_types` | `["text"]`, `["image"]`, or both | text |
> | `image_settings` | `max_results` (positive int), `caption` (bool) | none |
> 
> Related `include` values: `web_search_call.action.sources` (every URL consulted) and `web_search_call.results` (raw results, including image results). The search context window is capped at 128k tokens regardless of model. Use `tool_choice: "required"` to force a search; for multi-minute research runs, set `background: true`.
